    "text": "_Particle Markov Chain Monte Carlo Methods_ 281 \n\n\n\n<!-- Start of picture text -->\n4.2<br>4<br>3.8<br>σv 3.6<br>3.4<br>3.2<br>1.5<br>1.4<br>1.3<br>1.2<br>σw<br>1.1<br>1<br>0.9<br>3.2 3.4 3.6 3.8 4 4.2 0.9 1 1.1 1.2 1.3 1.4 1.5 10000 20000 30000 40000 50000<br>(a)<br>3.8<br>3.6<br>σv 3.4<br>3.2<br>3<br>2.8<br>1.4<br>1.3<br>1.2<br>1.1<br>σw<br>1<br>0.9<br>0.8<br>2.8 3 3.2 3.4 3.6 3.8 0.8 0.9 1 1.1 1.2 1.3 1.410000 20000 30000 40000 50000<br>(b)<br>3.8<br>3.6<br>σv 3.4<br>3.2<br>3<br>2.8<br>1.4<br>1.3<br>1.2<br>σw 1.1<br>1<br>0.9<br>0.8<br>2.8 3 3.2 3.4 3.6 3.8 0.8 0.9 1 1.1 1.2 1.3 1.4 10000 20000 30000 40000 50000<br>(c)<br><!-- End of picture text -->\n\n**Fig. 4.** Approximations of _p.σV_ j _y_ 1: _T /_ and _p.σW_ j _y_ 1: _T /_ , scatter plots and trace plots after burn-in of simulated values for (a) the MH one at a time update, (b) the PG sampler and (c) the PMMH sampler: , true values on the histograms; -------, true values on the trace plots; □, true values on the scatter plots \n\nhave an average acceptance rate of 0.80 for N = 2000 whereas it is equal to 0.27 for N = 200, resulting in a Markov chain which still mixes well. Given that the SMC proposal for N = 2000 is approximately 10 times more computationally expensive than for N = 200, it might seem appropriate to use N = 200 and to run more MCMC iterations. \n\nWhen _θ_ is unknown we set the prior _σ_ V<sup>2∼</sup><sup>_I_G.a,b/ and</sup><sup>_σ_</sup> W<sup>2∼</sup><sup>_I_G.a,b/ where</sup><sup>_I_G is the inverse</sup> \n\n"
